# maps/doors.map, with its behaviour written as TRIGGERS instead of as Doom
# line actions. Same geometry, to the vertex.
#
# This is the hand-authored proof that the trigger vocabulary can express a
# map that already works: door, lift, trip-wire and exit switch, against the
# same body proportions and the same spawns. maps/doors_trig.lua is the table;
# maps/doors.map keeps its doom.lua line table, and server/edge/test/doors.test.js
# still runs against it unchanged -- so the two implementations are compared on
# identical ground rather than one replacing the other.
#
# THE GEOMETRY IS NOT MAINTAINED TWICE. scripts/check_doors_twin.sh asserts this
# file and maps/doors.map are identical once comments and `script` lines are
# stripped, so an edit to one that is not made to the other fails the suite
# rather than quietly making the comparison meaningless.

sector floor 0 ceil 8 tag 1 light 200 floortex FLOOR4_8 ceiltex CEIL3_5
  vertex 0 0    wall tex STARTAN3
  vertex 10 0   wall tex STARTAN3
  vertex 10 3   neighbor 1 lower SW1COMP
  vertex 10 7   wall tex STARTAN3
  vertex 10 10  wall tex STARTAN3
  vertex 0 10   wall tex STARTAN3
end

# The door. Closed: its ceiling sits on its floor.
sector floor 0 ceil 0 tag 2 light 160 floortex FLOOR4_8 ceiltex CEIL3_5
  vertex 10 3  wall tex DOORSTOP
  vertex 12 3  neighbor 2
  vertex 12 7  wall tex DOORSTOP
  vertex 10 7  neighbor 0
end

sector floor 0 ceil 1.5 tag 3 light 200 floortex FLOOR4_8 ceiltex CEIL3_5
  vertex 12 0   wall tex STARTAN3
  vertex 17 0   neighbor 3 trigger
  vertex 17 10  wall tex STARTAN3
  vertex 12 10  wall tex STARTAN3
  vertex 12 7   neighbor 1
  vertex 12 3   wall tex STARTAN3
end

sector floor 0 ceil 1.5 tag 4 light 200 floortex FLOOR4_8 ceiltex CEIL3_5
  vertex 17 0   wall tex STARTAN3
  vertex 22 0   wall tex STARTAN3
  vertex 22 3   neighbor 4
  vertex 22 7   wall tex STARTAN3
  vertex 22 10  wall tex STARTAN3
  vertex 17 10  neighbor 2 trigger
end

# The lift, parked up at 3 so lowering it to its lowest neighbour is visible.
sector floor 3 ceil 8 tag 5 light 180 floortex FLOOR4_8 ceiltex CEIL3_5
  vertex 22 3  wall tex STARTAN3
  vertex 26 3  wall tex STARTAN3
  vertex 26 7  wall tex STARTAN3
  vertex 22 7  neighbor 3
end

# Doom's player at 1/48: a 16-unit radius and a 56-unit height. Stated here
# because the door's opening is 1.5 tall -- a Doom 72-unit doorway -- and the
# engine's default 2.0 body does not fit through it.
body radius 0.3333 height 1.1667

# What this map needs to behave. mapc copies each one next to the baked map and
# the server loads them from there -- no --script arguments required.
#
# scripts/doom.lua is DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and NOT because two scripts cannot
# share a hook -- they can, and that is settled: the VM keeps a LIST of handlers
# per hook and calls every one in load order, so doom.lua and triggers.lua would
# both be dispatched to correctly.
#
# The reason is overlap in what they DO. doom.lua's on_attack blows up a barrel
# on its own account, without consulting its line table at all, so a map loading
# both would have two scripts deciding what a hit barrel means -- each setting
# the animation, each clearing `solid`, each scheduling a despawn. Sharing a
# hook is safe; agreeing to disagree about the same prop is not, and no engine
# mechanism can settle that for a map author.
#
# maps/doors.map is where doom.lua is under test.
#
# The vocabulary must be declared BEFORE the runtime, which validates against
# it, and the runtime before the table, which is loaded into it. Out of order
# is an error that names the missing declaration.
script scripts/mob_vocab.lua
script scripts/mobs.lua
script mobs/doom_monsters.lua
script scripts/trigger_vocab.lua
script scripts/triggers.lua
script doors_trig.lua

spawn start 5 5 1
spawn behind_door 14 5 1
